act\").\n   - *Random token* suffixes at matched L (nonsensical baseline \u2014 controls for \"long garbage string\" effects).\n   - *Semantically coherent* suffix controls at matched L: refusal-begging prefix (\"Sure, here is how to\u2026\"), harmless-completion text, and persuasion/semantic jailbreak strings \u2014 controls for content vs length.\n\n2. Length ladder: L \u2208 {4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128} tokens (GCG-paper canonical length is ~20; ladder spans below and well above it). Log-spaced is defensible; optionally add midpoints near the estimated knee in a follow-up stage.\n\n3. Hold constant: optimization budget per suffix (e.g., 500 gradient+eval steps, identical for all L \u2014 flagged assumption, and a secondary arm with budget scaled \u221d L to disentangle budget from length, since fixing budget is itself confounded: longer suffixes get fewer updates per token); optimization loss (GCG native NLL on target \"Sure, here is\u2026\"); same 50 harmful AdvBench-style prompts; 3 random seeds per (family, L); decoding fixed (greedy or temp=0 with fixed max_new_tokens=256); white-box access to open-weight chat/instruct models. Models: 3 small models (e.g., Llama-2-7B-chat, Vicuna-7B, Mistral-7B-Instruct) + 1 black-box API model probed with transferred fixed-length GCG suffixes (secondary analysis only).\n\n4. ASR judging: primary = rule-based string matching excluding refusal strings (the standard in this literature); secondary = LLM-as-judge (e.g., a GPT-4-class or open Llama-Guard classifier) on all generations; report both + judge disagreement rate. Unit of analysis: prompt \u00d7 seed binary outcome; per-cell ASR over 150 runs; Wilson CIs.\n\n5. Length\u2013ASR model + saturation test (pre-registered):\n   - Fit per (model, family) with logistic mixed-effects: success ~ f(L) + (1|prompt) + (1|seed-run), comparing nested models: (a) linear/monotone in L, (b) saturating Hill/saturating-exponential with free plateau, (c) segmented regression with free breakpoint.